WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
In the eighteen sixties,

2
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:06.170
Dmitri Mendeleev set out to put
every known element in order.

3
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:10.650
His rule was simple: line them
up by the mass of their atoms.

4
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.170
Then he broke his own rule.

5
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.670
He swapped some pairs of
elements round, out of order.

6
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.970
And he left empty gaps in his table,

7
00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.350
where no element fitted at all.

8
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.750
How could breaking the pattern
make his table more right,

9
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:25.930
not less?

10
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.590
In this mission, you'll see
how Mendeleev used his gaps to

11
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.000
predict elements nobody had found yet.

12
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.650
You'll see how today's
table is ordered,

13
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.470
and what an element's group
tells you about its electrons.

14
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.910
Your mission: explain why
Mendeleev broke his own rule.
